// tests/languages.bx — every file in this repository that is not Burxt says why it is not.
//
//     burxt build tests/languages.bx -o languages
//     ./languages
//     ./languages --prove-it     # the negative control
//
// **Andre's rule for all three projects: if it can be written in Burxt, it must be, and reaching for
// another language is a gap report rather than a solution.** BMX was the furthest of the three from that
// rule and also had the strongest defence, which is exactly why the defence has to be written down per
// file instead of argued once. A count on its own reads worse than the truth; a count with a reason beside
// every line is an audit.
//
// **The counts live in the output, not in this header.** The first draft of this paragraph carried them —
// *"6,367 non-Burxt lines against 1,967 of Burxt"* — and by the time the ports were done that sentence had
// the ratio the wrong way round. A number in a header is a claim with no runner behind it, which is the
// defect this repository spends its checks converting; the one below it, printed on every run, is measured.
//
// The star-burxt session raised the rule after failing it themselves: told that `reference/bmx.js` exports
// `render`, they imported 1,171 lines of JavaScript into a product whose argument is that you do not need
// any, while `bmx_to_html` sat in `burxt/bmx.bx` already conformance-tested. Their amendment is worth
// keeping: *do not reimplement* is right, and *import the implementation in the host's own language when
// one exists* is stronger.
//
// **Six defences, and only the first two are about BMX being a format rather than a program:**
//
//   `reference`   The reference implementation is JavaScript ON PURPOSE. The format's central claim is that
//                 any language can implement it; a reference written in the host language would make that
//                 claim untestable by anyone who does not have the host.
//   `neutral`     A runner that takes an arbitrary implementation command. If it were Burxt, running the
//                 conformance suite would require the Burxt toolchain — so a stranger implementing BMX in
//                 a sixth language could not check their work, which is the one thing these files exist
//                 for. **The category is empty today and stays in the list**: it is the shape a future
//                 neutral runner would claim, and deleting an empty category is how the next one gets
//                 classified as something else.
//   `bootstrap`   `tools/check.sh` only. The entry point a contributor runs **before** any toolchain
//                 exists, so it cannot need what it installs — the chicken-and-egg category, and it
//                 already discloses which groups it skipped.
//   `platform`    The artefact under test, or the runtime it runs in, is JavaScript and nothing else can
//                 be: VS Code's extension host is Node, a TextMate grammar is tokenised by a JS engine,
//                 and `docs/assets/code.js` runs in the reader's browser.
//   `vendored`    A byte copy of a file from burxt-lang.org. `docs/_config.yml` says re-copy rather than
//                 edit, so it carries no marker and is named here instead — editing it to add one would
//                 break the property that makes it a copy.
//   `gap`         **Could be Burxt and is not.** Named rather than defended. This is the number the rule
//                 is about, and it is the only category that should ever shrink.
//
// **`standalone` used to be a category here and it was doing work it had not earned.** It covered sixteen
// files on the strength of `ci.yml`'s sentence — *the format must be testable without its first host
// installed* — and **that sentence is about the format, not about this repository's CI.** What the format's
// claim actually needs is the fixtures, which are data, plus a runner a stranger can execute: that is
// `neutral`, and it is genuinely permanent. `tests/branding.py`, `editors/vscode/pack.py` and the rest
// check *this repository*. In Burxt they make the conformance job need a toolchain — which costs a
// contributor without one some checks, and **costs the format's portability claim nothing at all.**
//
// Conflating those two is how a real property lent its authority to a choice. Reclassified 2026-08-21:
// fifteen files moved to `gap`, and the gap went from 708 lines to 3,074. **The number was wrong in the
// flattering direction, which is the only direction that survives.**
//
// **This total is not comparable to the other repositories', and that has to be said here or a reader will
// compare it anyway.** star-burxt reached `gap` zero; BMX cannot and should not. The difference is
// structural rather than a difference in effort: **`ci.yml`'s conformance job may not need a Burxt
// toolchain**, by a design decision older than this check — the format must be testable without its first
// host installed — and star has no equivalent job, so it has no equivalent constraint. That single property
// makes `reference`, `neutral` and `platform` **permanent** non-Burxt code here — a release cannot unforbid
// them and no amount of work should remove them.
//
// So the honest reading of these numbers is: `gap` is the debt, and the rest is the architecture. A
// different repository's zero and this one's remainder are not measuring the same thing. The star-burxt
// session put it better than I did — *"your standalone property generates permanent non-Burxt code and star
// has no equivalent, so BMX's remainder is not debt"* — and asked that it be written where the number is
// printed rather than left to inference.
//
// **The marker must be a DECLARATION, not a mention** — beginning a comment line, inside the file's first
// 20 lines, with its reason free to follow on the same line. `tests/version.bx` learned that the hard way
// the same day: its opt-out marker was matched anywhere in a file, so `CLAUDE.md` opted itself out of the
// version gate by *describing* how opting out works, and the page count fell silently. A file can be
// removed from a check by writing prose about the check, so this one is positional.
//
// ---- the port ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Ported from `tests/languages.py`, which was the last file in the ledger that was itself in the ledger.
// Byte-identical on the real tree, on the control, and on planted markers of every wrong shape.
use "std/files.bx";
use "std/os.bx";
use "std/string.bx";

const WITHIN: Int = 20;

// **Deliberately without `.bx`**: this counts what is NOT Burxt, and the Burxt total is measured
// separately below.
function suffixes() -> [String] {
    let mutable out: [String] = [];
    let a: Int = push(out, ".py");
    let b: Int = push(out, ".mjs");
    let c: Int = push(out, ".js");
    let d: Int = push(out, ".sh");
    return out;
}

function reasons() -> [String] {
    let mutable out: [String] = [];
    let a: Int = push(out, "reference");
    let b: Int = push(out, "neutral");
    let c: Int = push(out, "bootstrap");
    let d: Int = push(out, "platform");
    let e: Int = push(out, "gap");
    return out;
}

// The tuple as Python printed it, because the failure line quoted it verbatim.
const REASONS_SHOWN: String = "('reference', 'neutral', 'bootstrap', 'platform', 'gap')";

// Files that must not be edited, so they cannot carry a marker. The reason lives here because the
// alternative is editing the thing whose whole property is being an unedited copy.
const VENDORED: String = "docs/assets/site.js";

// Never scanned: git's own, installed dependencies, and the parser copy `pack.py` stages into the
// extension (gitignored, and counting it is what made an outside audit read 8,125 where the tree has
// 6,367 — the same 1,171 lines twice).
pure function skipped(rel: String) -> Bool {
    return string_starts_with(rel, ".git/") || string_contains(rel, "/.git/")
            || string_starts_with(rel, "node_modules/") || string_contains(rel, "/node_modules/")
            || string_starts_with(rel, "editors/vscode/reference/")
            || string_contains(rel, "/editors/vscode/reference/");
}

class Gap { path: String, lines: Int }

pure function has(items: [String], needle: String) -> Bool {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(items) {
        if items[i] == needle {
            return true;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return false;
}

pure function suffix_of(path: String) -> String {
    let mutable i: Int = len(path);
    while i > 0 {
        let b: Int = byte_at(path, i - 1);
        if b == 46 {
            return substring(path, i - 1, len(path) - i + 1);
        }
        if b == 47 {
            return "";
        }
        i -= 1;
    }
    return "";
}

// `len(text.splitlines())`: a trailing newline does not open a line.
pure function line_count(text: String) -> Int {
    if len(text) == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    let mutable n: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(text) {
        if byte_at(text, i) == 10 {
            n += 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    if byte_at(text, len(text) - 1) != 10 {
        n += 1;
    }
    return n;
}

pure function is_lower(b: Int) -> Bool {
    return b >= 97 && b <= 122;
}

// `^(?:#|//)\s*not-burxt:\s*([a-z]+)(?:\s|$)` against a STRIPPED line.
//
// **Anchored to the START of a comment line**, which is what keeps this a declaration rather than a
// mention: prose referring to a marker has words before it, so ``add `not-burxt: <reason>` `` in the
// message below cannot match itself. The first draft anchored the end immediately after the category and
// matched nothing but this very file, because every marker written carries its reason after an em dash — a
// predicate that only accepted the bare form, in a repository whose convention is that a marker explains
// itself.
pure function marker_on(raw: String) -> String {
    let line: String = string_trim(raw);
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    if string_starts_with(line, "#") {
        i = 1;
    } else {
        if string_starts_with(line, "//") {
            i = 2;
        } else {
            return "";
        }
    }
    while i < len(line) && string_is_space(byte_at(line, i)) {
        i += 1;
    }
    if !string_matches_at(line, "not-burxt:", i) {
        return "";
    }
    i += 10;
    while i < len(line) && string_is_space(byte_at(line, i)) {
        i += 1;
    }
    let start: Int = i;
    while i < len(line) && is_lower(byte_at(line, i)) {
        i += 1;
    }
    if i == start {
        return "";
    }
    if i < len(line) && !string_is_space(byte_at(line, i)) {
        return "";
    }
    return substring(line, start, i - start);
}

// The reason a file declares, as a declaration: alone on its line, near the top.
pure function declared(text: String) -> String {
    let lines: [String] = string_split(text, "\n");
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(lines) && i < WITHIN {
        let found: String = marker_on(lines[i]);
        if len(found) > 0 {
            return found;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return "";
}

pure function pad_left(text: String, width: Int) -> String {
    let mutable out: String = text;
    while char_count(out) < width {
        out = " " + out;
    }
    return out;
}

// ---- the program ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

let mutable prove: Bool = false;
let mutable a: Int = 1;
while a < os_arg_count() {
    if os_arg(a) == "--prove-it" {
        prove = true;
    }
    a += 1;
}

let all_reasons: [String] = reasons();
let exts: [String] = suffixes();
let mutable missing: [String] = [];
let mutable unknown: [String] = [];
let mutable tally_names: [String] = [];
let mutable tally_lines: [Int] = [];
let mutable gaps: [Gap] = [];
let mutable burxt: Int = 0;
let mutable stolen: Bool = false;

match file_walk(".") {
    None => {
        print_error("cannot walk the tree — run this from the repository root");
        exit(2);
    }
    Some(entries) => {
        let mutable i: Int = 0;
        while i < len(entries) {
            let mutable rel: String = entries[i];
            if string_starts_with(rel, "./") {
                rel = substring(rel, 2, len(rel) - 2);
            }
            // Every `.bx` in the tree, unfiltered, which is what the retired check summed.
            if string_ends_with(rel, ".bx") && file_is_file(rel) {
                match file_read_maybe(rel) {
                    None => { }
                    Some(text) => { burxt += line_count(text); }
                }
            }
            if has(exts, suffix_of(rel)) && file_is_file(rel) && !skipped(rel) {
                match file_read_maybe(rel) {
                    None => { }
                    Some(text) => {
                        let mutable reason: String = "";
                        if rel == VENDORED {
                            reason = "vendored";
                        } else {
                            reason = declared(text);
                        }
                        // The control is a real file with its marker taken away — the defect as it would
                        // happen, somebody adding a runner and not saying why it is not Burxt.
                        //
                        // **The subject is DISCOVERED, and it took two expiries to get here.** It named
                        // `tests/harness.py` until that file became `harness.bx`, at which point the
                        // control silently stopped having a subject and reported success. So it was
                        // repointed at `tests/languages.py` — this file — and porting this file to Burxt
                        // would have expired it again, because `.bx` is deliberately outside the scan.
                        // A control keyed to a filename expires exactly when the file is right to rename,
                        // and naming *itself* is not the fix when the fix is a rename. So it takes the
                        // first file the walk finds carrying a marker, whatever that file is.
                        if prove && !stolen && len(reason) > 0 {
                            stolen = true;
                            reason = "";
                        }
                        if len(reason) == 0 {
                            let m: Int = push(missing, rel);
                        } else {
                            if !has(all_reasons, reason) && reason != "vendored" {
                                let u: Int = push(unknown, rel + ": '" + reason + "'");
                            } else {
                                let lines: Int = line_count(text);
                                let mutable at: Int = -1;
                                let mutable t: Int = 0;
                                while t < len(tally_names) {
                                    if tally_names[t] == reason {
                                        at = t;
                                    }
                                    t += 1;
                                }
                                if at < 0 {
                                    let n: Int = push(tally_names, reason);
                                    let l: Int = push(tally_lines, lines);
                                } else {
                                    tally_lines[at] = tally_lines[at] + lines;
                                }
                                if reason == "gap" {
                                    let g: Int = push(gaps, Gap { path: rel, lines: lines });
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            i += 1;
        }
    }
}

let mutable k: Int = 0;
while k < len(missing) {
    print("  FAIL  " + missing[k] + " does not say why it is not Burxt — add `not-burxt: <reason>` in its"
            + " first " + to_string(WITHIN) + " lines");
    k += 1;
}
let mutable u: Int = 0;
while u < len(unknown) {
    print("  FAIL  " + unknown[u] + " is not one of " + REASONS_SHOWN);
    u += 1;
}

let mutable total: Int = 0;
let mutable t: Int = 0;
while t < len(tally_lines) {
    total += tally_lines[t];
    t += 1;
}
print("  " + to_string(burxt) + " lines of Burxt; " + to_string(total)
        + " not, every one of them accounted for:");
let mutable shown: [String] = [];
let mutable s: Int = 0;
while s < len(all_reasons) {
    let x: Int = push(shown, all_reasons[s]);
    s += 1;
}
let v: Int = push(shown, "vendored");
let mutable p: Int = 0;
while p < len(shown) {
    let mutable at: Int = -1;
    let mutable q: Int = 0;
    while q < len(tally_names) {
        if tally_names[q] == shown[p] {
            at = q;
        }
        q += 1;
    }
    if at >= 0 {
        print("    " + pad_left(to_string(tally_lines[at]), 5) + "  " + shown[p]);
    }
    p += 1;
}

// Printed, not asserted, for the reason in the header: `gap` is the debt and the rest is the
// architecture, so the two want reading differently rather than summing.
let mutable permanent: Int = 0;
let mutable r: Int = 0;
while r < len(tally_names) {
    if tally_names[r] == "reference" || tally_names[r] == "neutral" || tally_names[r] == "platform" {
        permanent += tally_lines[r];
    }
    r += 1;
}
print("  of that, " + to_string(permanent) + " lines are permanent — reference, neutral and platform,"
        + " which no");
print("  release and no amount of work can move; everything in `gap` can.");

// **The gap total is printed rather than capped.** A threshold here would be a number somebody raises
// when it is inconvenient, and the rule is not "keep it under N" — it is "know what it is".
if len(gaps) > 0 {
    print("  the gap, named:");
    // insertion sort, descending by size, stable — so files of equal length keep the walk's order
    let mutable g: Int = 1;
    while g < len(gaps) {
        let held: Gap = gaps[g];
        let mutable j: Int = g;
        while j > 0 && gaps[j - 1].lines < held.lines {
            gaps[j] = gaps[j - 1];
            j -= 1;
        }
        gaps[j] = held;
        g += 1;
    }
    let mutable n: Int = 0;
    while n < len(gaps) {
        print("    " + pad_left(to_string(gaps[n].lines), 5) + "  " + gaps[n].path);
        n += 1;
    }
}

// **Zero is not a passing count, and this printed its success line over nothing.** Proven by handing it an
// empty scan: *"0 lines not Burxt, every one of them accounted for"*, exit 0. A glob that stops matching —
// a renamed directory, a suffix dropped from the list — reads exactly like a tree with nothing left to
// explain. The star-burxt session found the same shape in the most important suite they have: `every
// guarantee holds`, no number, which is what it would print over zero guarantees.
//
// The floors are deliberately far below today's numbers. They are not budgets; they are the assertion that
// the scan found a tree at all.
if burxt < 500 || total < 500 {
    print("  FAIL  this scanned " + to_string(burxt) + " lines of Burxt and " + to_string(total)
            + " of anything else, which is not this repository — the glob has stopped finding it");
    if len(missing) == 0 {
        let m: Int = push(missing, "(the scan itself)");
    }
}

if prove {
    if len(missing) > 0 || len(unknown) > 0 {
        print("");
        print("the control failed as it must — a file with no stated reason is caught");
        exit(0);
    }
    print("");
    print("THE CONTROL DID NOT FAIL, so this check cannot see an unexplained language choice");
    exit(1);
}
if len(missing) > 0 || len(unknown) > 0 {
    print("");
    print(to_string(len(missing) + len(unknown)) + " file(s) do not account for their language");
    exit(1);
}
print("");
print("every non-Burxt file says why");
exit(0);
